#{[17592186317160 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ are infinite: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=+\\infty ,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=…,-6,-3,0,3,6,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/37/370ce6bc297340f28b1e5bcd4a7e0ca3ce5de6edac660e143ac70ba615184765.png)\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/9b/9b7758701595db784d2d9aeb59cc823316501748976ccbed41980fa29ad0a892.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\""] [17592186317161 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-6,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,1].$ Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $x=1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{3},…,$ so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-6,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [-6,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=-4$ and $x=6,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{4}^{-}}g(x)=0,\\,\\lim_{x\\to -{4}^{+}}g(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {6}^{-}}g(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {6}^{+}}g(x)=2\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-6,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[-6,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/9e/9e447c0672628ce8b304726f36885f18661bbba790ae34ca806aaae95a465bbb.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\""] [17592186317162 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/c9/c93cb4cc987ec951236c40a6e961a9eb9f180a594128d4b871692a140faa31e6.png)\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ are infinite: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}g(x)=+\\infty ,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}g(x)=-\\infty \n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$ Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{8},…,\n$$ so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ only.\""] [17592186317163 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$ Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{1}{16},…,\n$$ so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{-}}g(x)=+\\infty ,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{+}}g(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=2$ and $x=5,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}h(x)=0,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}h(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{-}}h(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{+}}h(x)=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\""] [17592186317209 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-\\frac{3}{5}$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {(-3/5)}^{-}}f(x)=7,$ then\n\n$f\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186317209 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-\\frac{3}{5}.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to (-3/5)}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)=\\lim_{x\\to (-3/5)}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {(-3/5)}^{-}}f(x)=7.\n$$"] [17592186317223 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3$ and $f(3)=-3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{x+(f(x))^{3}}{2}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186317224 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=6$ and $f(6)=14,$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {6}^{+}}\\sqrt{f(x)+{x}^{2}-1}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186317225 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$ and $f(-2)=2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}({x}^{3}+2(f(x))^{2})=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186317227 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=7$ and $f(7)=5,$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 7}\\frac{(f(x)+1)^{2}}{x-f(x)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186317256 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7cfd6da222fb36d06be1153ac0e398e567f8b9fb8cd1cd9e21f1bb35ed131f8d.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}(x+1)=-2+1=-1\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(-2)=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and III are false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/a5/a59fad69c9790a2b899c21fd730f779c28bc9b70f44a67851ad5ca92bff21951.png)"] [17592186317271 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/38/385029e7709a8d4eda45b101aba6f553c79ae3baef5b97080844e307a0b71cc7.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{true}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}(2x+3)=2(-3)+3=-3\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}((x-3)^{2}-3)=(3-3)^{2}-3=-3.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statement I is false.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/f4/f4bfe80cbcc0821e93012a859ccc91b3614fa7db2df8e25399fbadc3ef7a3355.png)"] [17592186317343 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ because $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/77/7736417b068541a1609efa46e9824cd3f65cd34856c1d2f5d0ec7258ec883b5c.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ because ${F}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}(2x+1)=2(-3)+1=-5\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and II are false.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/ac/ac4d89fcccca99bebaeab4b44baac9c5c03651c38cef7e4d14cfe57978d066b7.png)"] [17592186317367 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\frac{7}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186317367 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x):$ \n\n![](images/04/04dd8257ef32291b930aa29f5bed4de2c41761692fd48d08ed2479f6cbd78659.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\frac{7}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x))=\\frac{7}{\\pi }\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=-\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186317370 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }(10\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186317370 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3):$ \n\n![](images/bb/bb49c052cabe12e8869270ab61b1de9e7f45b6acd6eaa9bc0d4c6892446a7cb6.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }(10\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3))=(10\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=5\\pi .\n$$"] [17592186317467 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186317470 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)-\\sqrt{3}}{2}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186319070 :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[((\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{3}+2($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]$"] [17592186319071 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[({2}^{\\,2+3k/n}+1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186319071 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{3}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{3k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={2}^{\\,{x}_{k}}+1.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}({2}^{x}+1)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[({2}^{\\,2+3k/n}+1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)={2}^{x}+1\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$"] [17592186319077 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[5{(2+\\frac{4k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186319077 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=6.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=5{x}_{k}^{3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{6}5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[5{(2+\\frac{4k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=6.\n$$"] [17592186319081 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{((-1+\\frac{3k}{n})+8)}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186319081 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{3}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{n}=\\frac{b-(-1)}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=-1+\\frac{3k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=({x}_{k}+8)^{1/3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(x+8)^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{((-1+\\frac{3k}{n})+8)}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+8)^{1/3}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=2.\n$$"] [17592186319082 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{2+\\frac{3k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186319082 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{3}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{3k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=\\sqrt{{x}_{k}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{2+\\frac{3k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$"] [17592186319085 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$"] [17592186319214 :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{-8n+7}{4n}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186319215 :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{5{n}^{2}+7}{3{n}^{3}-2}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186319216 :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{2-7{n}^{3}}{4{n}^{4}+n}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186319218 :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(5{n}^{2}-4)(5{n}^{2}+4)}{{n}^{2}(3-40{n}^{2})}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186319219 :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(3{n}^{3}-5)(3{n}^{3}+5)}{{n}^{3}(2-27{n}^{3})}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186319220 :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(2{n}^{2}-3)(2{n}^{2}+3)}{{n}^{2}(5-12{n}^{3})}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186320803 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{5}+4).$"] [17592186320929 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(5{x}^{3}).$"] [17592186320986 :question/problem "![](images/67/67b77d2d3798e87593c7dddeea93603fff36d9153f53248cc2dab295f4879321.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)}.$"] [17592186321158 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=1"] [17592186321178 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=-1"] [17592186321208 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=0"] [17592186321220 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=2"] [17592186321348 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [17592186321392 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=0"] [17592186321454 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [17592186321520 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=1"] [17592186321531 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1"] [17592186321683 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=4"] [17592186321893 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=2"] [17592186321926 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1"] [17592186322001 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=2"] [17592186322076 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=2"] [17592186322110 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-2"] [17592186322223 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=2"] [17592186322279 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=2"] [17592186322343 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=1"] [17592186322361 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=-4"] [17592186322449 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)=0"] [17592186322578 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=-8"] [17592186322633 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)=1"] [17592186322685 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)=2"] [17592186322722 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)=\\sqrt{2}"] [17592186322728 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [17592186322816 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)=4"] [17592186322826 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=1"] [17592186322907 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=4"] [17592186322977 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=3"] [17592186323130 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=4"] [17592186323752 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz}=$"] [17592186323753 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy-2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-xy}=\n$$"] [17592186323899 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /3)}({tan}^{2}⁡(x)+3{cot}^{2}⁡(x)).$"] [17592186323924 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}{6{x}^{3}-9}=\n$$"] [17592186323933 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{4}-1}{x-1}=$"] [17592186323934 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{5{x}^{2}-3x-14}{{x}^{2}+x-6}=$"] [17592186323941 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{x+16}=$"] [17592186323942 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{3}-6}{{x}^{2}-4}=$"] [17592186323956 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$ and $f(-1)=-3,$ then what is $\\lim_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)?$"] [17592186323956 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=f(-1)=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=-3.\n$$"] [17592186323960 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}f(x)=4,$ then what is $f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)?$"] [17592186323960 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}f(x)=4.\n$$"] [17592186326681 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “The function is left-continuous at $x=0$” is false.  The function is not left-continuous at $x=0,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=3\\ne 0=f(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=0$” is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=0,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=0=f(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is continuous at $x=0$” is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=0,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=0$” is true."] [17592186326691 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$” is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$” is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is continuous at $x=-1$” is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."] [17592186326710 :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(2)=2$ exists, $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=3.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, “It has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$” is true, and “It has a point discontinuity at $x=2.$” and “It is continuous at $x=2.$” are false."] [17592186327014 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist"] [17592186327019 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist"] [17592186327169 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist"] [17592186327175 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)"] [175921863280